### What is the Tasks Scheduling Order problem?

Given a number of tasks and prerequisite pairs, return one valid order in which all tasks can be completed. If no valid order exists (due to a cycle), return an empty list. This extends the feasibility variant by recording the actual sequence.

```java
package com.bank.onboarding;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Queue;

public class TaskSchedulingOrder {

    public static List<Integer> findOrder(int taskCount, int[][] prerequisites) {
        List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
        int[] inDegree = new int[taskCount];

        for (int i = 0; i < taskCount; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());

        for (int[] pre : prerequisites) {
            adj.get(pre[0]).add(pre[1]);
            inDegree[pre[1]]++;
        }

        Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < taskCount; i++) {
            if (inDegree[i] == 0) queue.add(i);
        }

        List<Integer> order = new ArrayList<>();
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            int task = queue.poll();
            order.add(task); // record the order
            for (int next : adj.get(task)) {
                if (--inDegree[next] == 0) {
                    queue.add(next);
                }
            }
        }

        return order.size() == taskCount ? order : new ArrayList<>();
    }
}
```

### How does recording the order differ from the feasibility check?

The feasibility variant just counts placed tasks and returns a boolean. The order variant replaces the counter with a list that records each task as it leaves the queue. The rest of the algorithm is identical. This list is the topological order.

```java
// Feasibility: placed++; ... return placed == taskCount;
// Order:       order.add(task); ... return order.size() == taskCount ? order : empty;
```

### Why is the removal order a valid completion order?

A task enters the queue only when its in-degree reaches 0, meaning all its prerequisites have already been removed and placed in the order. So every task in the order appears after all its prerequisites, which is exactly the definition of a valid topological order.

```java
package com.bank.onboarding;

public class OrderCorrectnessDemo {

    public static void demonstrate() {
        // Prerequisites: 0->1, 0->2, 1->3, 2->3
        // Task 0 has no prerequisites → enters queue first
        // Tasks 1,2 wait for 0 → enter after 0 is placed
        // Task 3 waits for 1 and 2 → enters after both are placed
        // Order: [0, 1, 2, 3] or [0, 2, 1, 3] (both valid)

        System.out.println(TaskSchedulingOrder.findOrder(4,
            new int[][]{{0,1},{0,2},{1,3},{2,3}}));
    }
}
```

```text
[0, 1, 2, 3]
```

### What happens when a cycle exists?

When a cycle exists, tasks in the cycle never reach in-degree 0, so they never enter the queue and never get placed. The order list ends up shorter than the total task count, and we return an empty list.

```java
package com.bank.onboarding;

public class CycleDemo {

    public static void demonstrate() {
        // Cycle: 0->1, 1->2, 2->0
        // In-degrees: [1, 1, 1] → no task has in-degree 0!
        // Queue is empty from the start
        // Order: [] (empty), order.size()=0 != 3 → return empty

        System.out.println(TaskSchedulingOrder.findOrder(3,
            new int[][]{{0,1},{1,2},{2,0}})); // []
    }
}
```

```text
[]
```

### Why can multiple valid orders exist?

When multiple tasks have in-degree 0 at the same time, any of them can be processed next. The queue ordering (FIFO) picks one, but a different ordering would also be valid. For example, with prerequisites 0->2 and 1->2, both orders [0, 1, 2] and [1, 0, 2] are valid.

```java
package com.bank.onboarding;

public class MultipleOrders {

    public static void demonstrate() {
        // Prerequisites: 0->2, 1->2
        // Both 0 and 1 have in-degree 0
        // FIFO queue: processes 0 first, then 1, then 2
        // But [1, 0, 2] is equally valid

        System.out.println(TaskSchedulingOrder.findOrder(3,
            new int[][]{{0,2},{1,2}})); // [0, 1, 2]
    }
}
```

```text
[0, 1, 2]
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Aspect | Complexity | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Time | O(V + E) | Each task and edge processed once |
| Space | O(V + E) | Adjacency list + in-degree array + queue + order list |

### What are edge cases?

Edge cases include zero tasks (return empty list), tasks with no prerequisites (return any permutation), and a single task (return [0]).

```java
package com.bank.onboarding;

public class EdgeCases {

    public static void test() {
        System.out.println(TaskSchedulingOrder.findOrder(0, new int[][]{})); // []
        System.out.println(TaskSchedulingOrder.findOrder(3, new int[][]{})); // [0, 1, 2]
        System.out.println(TaskSchedulingOrder.findOrder(1, new int[][]{})); // [0]
    }
}
```

```text
[]
[0, 1, 2]
[0]
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is returning the incomplete order instead of empty when a cycle is detected. Another mistake is not handling the case where the task count is zero.

```java
package com.bank.onboarding;

public class CommonMistakes {

    // WRONG: returning incomplete order when cycle exists
    // If order.size() < taskCount, returning order gives a partial result
    // Fix: check order.size() == taskCount before returning

    // WRONG: not handling zero tasks
    // taskCount=0 should return [] (empty, but valid)
}
```

### How is this used in banking?

At the National Bank of India, the onboarding team generates a corporate onboarding playbook where document verification precedes account opening, account opening precedes card issuance, and KYC precedes loan review. The operations team needs the ordered steps, not just a yes/no feasibility answer.

```java
package com.bank.onboarding;

import java.util.List;

public class OnboardingPlaybook {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 0: KYC Verification
        // 1: Account Opening
        // 2: Card Issuance
        // 3: Loan Review
        int[][] prerequisites = {{0,1}, {1,2}, {0,3}};
        List<Integer> order = TaskSchedulingOrder.findOrder(4, prerequisites);

        String[] taskNames = {"KYC Verification", "Account Opening",
                              "Card Issuance", "Loan Review"};
        System.out.println("Onboarding playbook:");
        for (int i = 0; i < order.size(); i++) {
            System.out.println((i + 1) + ". " + taskNames[order.get(i)]);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Onboarding playbook:
1. KYC Verification
2. Account Opening
3. Loan Review
4. Card Issuance
```
